{"format":1,"seq":57,"writes":[{"type":"document.change","id":2,"content":{"version":3,"kind":"delta","ops":[["p",["generation","message","content"],1,0,[{"type":"text","text":""}]],["a",["generation","message","content",0,"thinkingSignature"],""],["a",["generation","message","content",1,"text"],"Mostly yes. It's a short, sensible process, and the tags and GitHub Releases so far look right. The written instructions are the weak part: they're thinner than what you actually do, and in a few places they disagree with it. I didn't change any files; this is just the list.\n\n**What's wrong or inconsistent now**\n\n- **Tag type.** Every existing tag, including `v2` and `v2.2.x`, is an annotated tag. The commands in this PR (`git tag v2.3.0`, `git tag -f v2 v2.3.0`) make lightweight ones. CONTRIBUTING doesn't say which kind to use.\n- **Releases via PR or straight to main.** Releases 2.2.1 and 2.2.2 were committed straight to `main` (no PR number in the commit). This one is a PR. That's why \"check passes on `main`\" (CONTRIBUTING) and \"passes on this branch\" (this PR) disagree, and why \"tag the commit\" is unclear: with squash merges, the commit to tag is the merge"]]}}]}